Toward a Context-Sensitive Theory of Spiritual Music and Altered Experience
A Competing-Models, Dynamic and Two-Tier Framework for Moroccan Ritual Music

Ouadi Maakoul + SI TEAM 

Theoretical Doctoral Dissertation — Draft 1.0 (derived from Research Framework V0.4) — October 2026

Abstract

Intense musical and ritual experiences — devotional absorption, aesthetic emotion, trance and possession — recur across human cultures, and Moroccan Gnawa, Issawa and Sufi practices are among the richest settings in which to study them. Existing accounts fall into three families: mechanistic accounts that explain experience by acoustic or neural drive, social accounts that explain it by coordination and bonding, and cultural accounts that explain it by meaning, role and ritual legitimacy. Each family is plausible; few studies force them to compete under shared outcome definitions and out-of-sample tests.
This dissertation develops a context-sensitive theory that treats these families as six explicitly competing models (M1 preference, M2 predictive processing, M3 social coordination, M4 specified interactions, M5 cultural legitimacy, M6 bottom-up entrainment) evaluated on a prespecified nested ladder. It makes predictive processing computable through participant-validated surprisal and entropy, formalizes a dated hypothesis (a bounded inverted-U) with a formal U-shape test, and introduces a one-dimensional dynamic generative model whose distinctive prediction is state dependence with hysteresis, separating experiential entry, maintenance and exit. It splits inference into two tiers: controlled laboratory study of devotional and musical absorption (T1, T2), and ethically bounded naturalistic study of trance-related and possession-related experience (T3, T4). It specifies measurement development, invariance testing, synchrony controls, explicit estimands for embodied confounds, simulation-based design analysis, decision rules and ethics.
The contribution is methodological and theoretical: a framework in which simpler explanations are given a fair chance to win, in which context is represented by measured variables rather than invoked post hoc, and in which the limits of cross-cultural and cross-tier inference are stated in advance. Metaphysical questions are explicitly separated from, and left unresolved by, the empirical programme.
Keywords: spiritual music; ritual; trance; possession; Gnawa; Issawa; Sufism; dhikr; predictive processing; musical surprisal; entrainment; synchrony; hysteresis; measurement invariance; competing models; Bayesian hierarchical models.
Reader's Guide and Conventions
Evidence labels used throughout:
• [E1] peer-reviewed empirical study
• [E2] review-level synthesis
• [E3] ethnographic or theoretical monograph
• [E4] conference abstract, talk, preprint or popular-press report (weakest; never used as stand-alone support)
• [H] hypothesis proposed by this dissertation
• [V] bibliographic details or content not yet verified against a primary record
Notation. Mathematical expressions are written in plain Unicode. A full notation table is in Appendix A. Subscripts: i = participant, p = performance, g = group, c = community, t = phase or time index, k = measurement indicator.
Three levels of explanation (kept separate throughout):
• Level 1 — empirical mechanisms: what changes in attention, affect, physiology, behaviour and reported experience.
• Level 2 — cultural and existential meaning: how participants and communities interpret those changes.
• Level 3 — metaphysical interpretation: whether any interpretation is true. This dissertation does not address Level 3.
Part I — Foundations
Chapter 1. Introduction
1.1 The problem
Humans across cultures use sound, rhythm, repetition and collective vocalization to express suffering, gratitude, devotion and wonder. In Moroccan settings, an invocation such as La ilaha illa Allah may be repeated within a musical and collective frame for long periods. Participants may report calm attention, tears, joy, relief, belonging, trance, or an encounter interpreted as divine or spiritual presence. The question motivating this work is not whether such experiences are "real" in a metaphysical sense but how, for whom and at which stage of an experience the observable components of the practice contribute to it.
1.2 Three puzzles
1. Content-poor repetition, content-rich experience. Repetitive material can accompany profound experience. Literal meaning alone cannot explain this; rhythm, familiarity, social participation, memory and ritual legitimacy are all candidate contributors whose relative weights are unknown.
2. Similar reports, different practices. Different traditions may yield subjectively comparable reports (absorption, release, connectedness) while differing in doctrine, history, musical structure and ritual function. Similarity of report does not establish similarity of mechanism or meaning.
3. Mechanism is not meaning. A physiological or computational account of how an experience arises does not settle what it means to those who undergo it, nor whether their interpretation is true.
1.3 Research questions
• RQ1 (typology). Do participants in the studied communities distinguish devotional absorption, musical absorption, trance-related experience and possession-related experience, and how do they describe the distinctions?
• RQ2 (competing explanations). Which of six explanation families — preference, predictive processing, social coordination, specified interactions, cultural legitimacy, bottom-up entrainment — contributes reliable incremental predictive value for which experiential outcomes?
• RQ3 (predictive processing). Can participant-validated musical surprisal and entropy be constructed for Moroccan ritual music, and do they predict absorption beyond acoustic features and preference? Is the relationship non-monotonic?
• RQ4 (dynamics). Is experience state-dependent, so that identical musical input produces different responses depending on preceding experiential state, and do the conditions for entry, maintenance and exit differ (hysteresis)?
• RQ5 (context). Do role, tradition, setting, familiarity and prior-measured legitimacy improve held-out prediction beyond acoustic, predictive and social variables?
• RQ6 (inference limits). Which conclusions can laboratory evidence (Tier A) and which can naturalistic field evidence (Tier B) support, and when are cross-tradition comparisons licensed by measurement invariance?
1.4 Thesis statement and central claims
The thesis is that intense musical and ritual experience is best studied as a context-dependent, temporally structured process whose candidate mechanisms should be compared under common outcome definitions, prespecified validation and explicitly bounded inference. Five claims follow.
• C1. Experience categories (T1–T4) must be grounded in participants' concepts before they are measured, and must not be assumed universal.
• C2. The principal explanation families make different predictive commitments and can be forced to compete.
• C3. Predictive processing becomes testable only when its predictors are computable, participant-validated and fixed before outcomes are seen.
• C4. The distinctive contribution of a dynamic account is state dependence (and possibly hysteresis), which static models cannot represent.
• C5. Laboratory and field evidence answer different questions and are not pooled.
1.5 What the dissertation does not claim
• It does not claim that music causes any spiritual state, or that any one mechanism dominates universally.
• It does not claim that laboratory absorption reproduces ritual trance or possession.
• It does not treat Gnawa, Issawa and Sufi practices as equivalent, nor as internally homogeneous.
• It does not treat possession as pathology or as evidence for or against supernatural entities.
• It does not claim cross-tradition convergence; convergence is a hypothesis to be tested subject to invariance.
• It does not claim that physiological findings resolve metaphysical questions.
1.6 Scope
The initial empirical focus is Moroccan spiritual musical traditions, including Gnawa and Issawa practices and Sufi dhikr, where community access, consent and appropriate expertise permit research. Findings from other traditions (for example shamanic drumming) are comparative evidence only; their transfer to Morocco requires an explicit argument.
1.7 Contributions
1. Conceptual: a typology (T1–T4) that is provisional, emic-first and non-hierarchical.
2. Methodological: a nested model-comparison ladder with participant-validated predictive-processing variables, formal U-shape testing, leave-future-out validation and explicit total-effect versus conditional estimands.
3. Theoretical: a threshold-based dynamic generative model that gives hysteresis a precise definition and a distinctive, falsifiable prediction.
4. Design: a two-tier architecture that separates what the laboratory and the field can each license.
5. Ethical: a protocol-level treatment of sacred settings, recording, data sovereignty and positionality.
1.8 Structure
Part I (Chapters 1–3) sets foundations. Part II (Chapters 4–8) develops the theory: framework, competing models, predictive processing, the inverted-U hypothesis, and the dynamic model. Part III (Chapters 9–12) specifies the research programme: designs, measurement, confounds and analysis. Part IV (Chapters 13–16) covers falsification, ethics, roadmap and contributions. Appendices give notation, a preregistration skeleton, a glossary, the evidence audit, the simulation protocol and the verification list.
Chapter 2. Background and Literature Audit
This chapter is a starting audit, not a completed systematic review. A full review is Phase 1 of the roadmap (Chapter 15).
2.1 Music and trance: three positions
• Non-determinism (Rouget, 1985 [E3]). Surveying world ethnography, Rouget concluded that the relation between music and trance is neither causal nor deterministic. Music is not a switch for trance; the same sound can accompany trance in one setting and not in another. This is the strongest challenge to any simple "acoustics → experience" arrow and motivates the present dissertation's insistence on context variables and on rival models.
• Psychophysiological contrast (Becker, 2004 [E3/E1]). Becker compared physiological and emotional responses of religious "ecstatics" and secular "deep listeners", proposing that a disposition to deep listening or trancing connects across settings. Becker's work motivates the individual-difference and role components but is not, by itself, adequate support for specific claims about synchrony or reward pathways.
• Embodied ethnography (Kapchan, 2007 [E3]). From participant ethnography of Gnawa practice, Kapchan argues that trance cannot be fully explained by Becker's model and reads Gnawa trance as an enactment of embodied memory of subjugation, with agency transferred to a possessing spirit and experienced as release and catharsis. This motivates treating possession and trance as culturally and historically situated rather than as a generic altered state.
The three positions are not mutually exclusive. They differ in where they locate explanatory weight: in the relation of sound to state (Rouget), in the individual (Becker), and in history and embodiment (Kapchan).
2.2 Moroccan traditions are not interchangeable
• Gnawa. A ritual and healing tradition associated with all-night ceremonies, specific instruments and repertoires organized by ritual sequence, in which possession-related and trance-related experiences are locally recognized. Ethnographic literature links it to histories of slavery and to practices of healing [E3].
• Issawa. A Sufi brotherhood tradition with communal ritual music and recitation. Details of organization, repertoire and ritual variation must be established ethnographically [V]. In preliminary (non-systematic) searches for this dissertation, no Issawa-specific empirical study of experience or physiology was located; whether this is a true gap must be verified in Phase 1.
• Sufi dhikr. Remembrance of God through verbal or silent repetition, with or without movement and music; communal forms differ across orders [E3].
Gnawa ritual trance and healing must not be equated with dhikr merely because both involve repetition, religious meaning and collective performance. Within each tradition, variation across groups, regions, performers and ritual phases is theoretically important.
2.3 Predictive processing and music
A family of accounts holds that musical pleasure and engagement arise from the interplay of expectation, prediction error and uncertainty. Pleasure is often described as peaking at intermediate levels of complexity or predictability (an inverted-U) [E1/E2]. Commentary in this tradition frames music as following from prediction-error minimization (Kraus & Hesselmann, 2021 [E2]) and links predictive processing, rhythmic entrainment, social bonding and implicit learning (Siman-Tov and colleagues [E1][V year/details]).
Two further proposals are relevant but unverified: that synchrony is rewarding because synchronized action is predictable and reduces uncertainty across timescales (Bamford [E4], conference abstract), and that rhythmic synchrony relaxes high-level social priors, making them more revisable and thereby linking rhythm, ritual change and trance (a 2026 review known to this project through a secondary summary [E4][V]). Both are treated as candidate ideas, not established findings.
The inverted-U is a candidate model, not a law. Its form, its dependence on listener enculturation, and its applicability to ritual contexts are open.
2.4 Social coordination and reward
Review-level work reports that music engages social-affiliation systems, that interpersonal synchrony signals affiliation, that music-induced movement increases closeness, and that endogenous opioid systems modulate both social bonding and musical pleasure (Nummenmaa, Putkinen & Sams, 2021 [E2]). The generalization to Moroccan ritual is unverified. Field reports of heart-rate synchrony in a Sufi dhikr congregation and in fire-walking rituals exist, but the dhikr report was described in secondary sources as new, unpublished research [E4]; such reports are hypothesis-generating and must be treated cautiously, particularly because shared stimulus, movement and breathing can produce apparent synchrony without interpersonal coupling.
2.5 Rhythm-induced altered states
• A recent review of trance neuroscience concludes that evidence is fragmented and methodologically heterogeneous [E2][V authors/details].
• A 2025 narrative review proposes that rhythmic sound may induce altered states via entrainment of thalamo-cortical pathways to low-frequency activity (Aparicio-Terrés and colleagues [E2]).
• Work with experienced shamanic practitioners, reported in a talk abstract, describes brain-network reconfiguration consistent with perceptual decoupling, with drum sounds largely gated out during trance (Hove [E4][V]). If this generalizes, surprisal-type stimulus processing could predict entry into trance but not its maintenance.
• A single-group Sufi breathing-and-meditation QEEG case study reports before/after differences over ten weeks [E1, very weak]. It is not used as support in this dissertation.
None of these studies concern Gnawa or Issawa ritual directly. Transfer requires argument and, ultimately, data.
2.6 Evidence summary and gap statement
• Strong: contextual non-determinism of the music–trance relation; ethnographic richness of Moroccan traditions.
• Moderate: music engages expectation and social-reward processes in general; rhythmic stimulation affects arousal and neural activity.
• Weak or absent for the present target: participant-validated predictive measures for Moroccan ritual music; physiological and experiential time series from Gnawa/Issawa ritual; measurement invariance of experience constructs across these traditions; direct comparison of the six explanation families under shared validation.
The gap is not a lack of ideas but a lack of adjudication.
Chapter 3. Constructs and Typology
3.1 Principles
1. Emic before etic. Categories begin from participants' terms. Researcher categories are provisional analytic labels.
2. Distinct measurement. Perceived unity, belonging, devotion, release and trance are measured separately, not collapsed into one assumed state.
3. No hierarchy. The categories are not ranks of spiritual depth.
4. Overlap permitted. An episode may involve several categories, or none cleanly.
3.2 The four provisional domains
• T1 — Devotional absorption. Sustained attention or involvement associated with devotional practice, as defined by participants. Altered consciousness is not required.
• T2 — Musical or aesthetic absorption. Intense involvement in listening or performing, possibly with chills, tears, pleasure, release or reduced awareness of surroundings, without requiring a devotional interpretation.
• T3 — Trance-related experience. A locally recognized alteration in attention, awareness, perception, bodily experience or sense of agency. Characteristics and causes are established per setting.
• T4 — Possession-related experience. An experience interpreted within a tradition through its concepts of possession, spiritual presence or related ideas. Participants' interpretations are documented without being treated as established scientific explanations and without being dismissed as errors.
These are not diagnoses and not claims about the objective existence of supernatural entities.
3.3 Related constructs kept distinct
• Attentional concentration: allocation and maintenance of attention on a stimulus or object. It is not reduced mental activity and not an altered state.
• Emotional regulation: change in intensity, duration, expression or interpretation of emotion; not necessarily calming.
• Collective spiritual experience: subjective experience during shared practice, including perceived unity, belonging, devotion or release; each facet is measured separately.
• Theological interpretation: the meaning participants assign to experience in relation to God, unity, creation or other concepts. It is an object of historical, philosophical and psychological study, not a physiological variable.
3.4 Status of the typology
Before any formal measurement, ethnography and cognitive interviewing must establish (a) whether participants recognize these distinctions, (b) what terminology they use, and (c) which experiences resist classification. The typology is revised if participants' own categories diverge (for example, if a locally important category has no counterpart among T1–T4).
3.5 The treatment of possession
Possession-related experience is analysed as a culturally situated experience. The research does not classify it as pathology, does not take it as independent evidence for a metaphysical claim, and does not require participants to accept the researchers' framing. Where participants consider a recording or measurement during possession inappropriate, the study does not record it (Chapter 14).
3.6 Relation to the three levels
T1–T4 are defined at the interface of Levels 1 and 2. The empirical programme addresses Level 1 mechanisms and Level 2 meaning; any Level 3 inference requires separate philosophical argument and is outside scope. Neither the intensity of an experience nor a mechanistic explanation of it independently proves or disproves a theological claim.
Part II — Theory
Chapter 4. The Framework
4.1 Overview
The framework has six interacting components. They are analytic categories, not claimed causal modules; their relations are empirical questions.
• A. Musical structure: rhythm, repetition, tempo, melodic and timbral features, vocal organization, improvisation, intensity change.
• B. Predictive processing: learned expectations, event surprisal and uncertainty, evaluated against participant-specific expectations rather than presumed universal rules.
• C. Embodied participation: breathing, movement, posture, vocal exertion and other bodily processes accompanying musical participation.
• D. Social coordination and reward: interpersonal coordination, collective participation, perceived closeness and musical reward. Synchrony alone is not proof of social bonding.
• E. Cultural and ritual organization: tradition, role, setting, ritual sequence, historical memory and legitimacy established before outcomes are measured.
• F. Experiential outcomes: attention, absorption, affect, perceived agency, reported altered experience and locally meaningful interpretations, each assessed separately.
No component is assumed necessary or sufficient for every outcome, and none dominates universally.
4.2 Formal setup
The unit of observation is the participant–episode (or participant–phase) indexed by i (participant), p (performance), g (group), c (community) and t (phase or time index). The principal variable families are:
• m_it — musical features (component A), including rhythmic complexity.
• S_it, H_it — surprisal and predictive entropy (component B) from a participant-validated sequence model.
• b_it — bodily variables: breathing rate, movement, vocal exertion, arousal (component C).
• q_ip — social participation and coordination variables (component D).
• k_ip — context variables: role, tradition, setting, familiarity, prior legitimacy (component E).
• z_it — core confounders and covariates: sleep, fatigue, time of day, ritual phase, exposures, preference.
• a_it — latent absorption (a hypothesized latent state; Chapter 8).
• y_itk — observed outcome indicators k (component F).
4.3 Causal architecture (candidate)
The following is a candidate set of relationships, not an established causal model. In words:
1. Musical structure (m) determines, together with the listener's learned model, the sequence of surprisal and entropy values (S, H). Context and familiarity (k) shape the listener's model and thereby which values are experienced.
2. Musical structure, surprisal/entropy, bodily state (b) and social participation (q) jointly influence the latent state (a). Bodily state and social participation also influence each other and are influenced by the music.
3. The latent state at time t depends on its own earlier value (persistence), and may feed back on bodily state and on subsequent attention to the music. The architecture is therefore recursive: expectations and bodily states alter responses to the music, while the unfolding performance alters expectations, interaction and experience.
4. Fatigue, fasting, heat and similar exposures (z) affect bodily state and the latent state, and are sometimes consequences of participation; their causal role must be fixed before analysis (Chapter 11).
5. The latent state generates observed indicators (y) and, via cultural interpretation (k), the meanings participants report.
6. Prior legitimacy (part of k) is measured before the target experience to prevent circularity; post-experience legitimacy ratings are treated as outcomes.
A path diagram of these relations is to be drawn as a directed acyclic graph over time-indexed variables in Phase 4 (Appendix E).
4.4 Assumptions
• A1 Provisional constructs. T1–T4 are provisional until ethnography confirms them.
• A2 Heterogeneity. Effects vary by role, tradition, setting and individual; pooled average effects are not presumed.
• A3 Participant-specific expectation. Listeners' predictive models differ and must be estimated, not assumed.
• A4 Temporal dependence. Observations within an episode are not independent; independent replication is counted at the level of performances and communities.
• A5 Measurement is imperfect. Indicators are noisy, retrospective in part, and may not be comparable across traditions.
• A6 Observational limits. Field associations are not causal unless an identification strategy is defended.
• A7 Neutrality. The empirical programme is neutral about Level 3.
4.5 Estimands
Because components C and D can lie on causal pathways, each analysis states its estimand.
• Predictive estimand: expected held-out predictive accuracy of an outcome given a predictor set, with its uncertainty. This is the primary estimand for model comparison (Chapter 5).
• Total-effect estimand (Tier A): the effect of a manipulated musical feature or condition on an outcome, including effects mediated by bodily response.
• Conditional (direct-pathway) estimand: the association conditional on specified bodily variables, interpreted only under stated assumptions.
• Contextual association (Tier B): adjusted associations between context variables and outcomes; not causal without additional identification.
4.6 Entry, maintenance and exit
With a_it the latent absorption level and a preregistered high-state definition (a_it above a calibrated threshold, or a classification from the measurement model):
• Entry: transition from a low to a high state.
• Maintenance: persistence of the high state over subsequent phases.
• Exit or decline: a reduction in the latent measure.
Operational definitions are fixed after ethnography and pilot data, not chosen to fit the model.
Chapter 5. Competing Explanatory Models
5.1 Principle
Models differ in predictive commitments, not just in number of variables. All are evaluated on the same prespecified outcome definitions, core confounders and validation partitions.
5.2 The six models
M1 — Preference. Explanation: musical features and individual preference. Predictors: preregistered acoustic features, preference ratings. Distinctive prediction: acoustics and preference predict outcomes across settings, with limited incremental benefit from higher-level mechanisms. Disconfirmed if: later blocks add reliable held-out gain.
M2 — Predictive processing. Explanation: learned expectations and sequence predictability. Predictors: participant-validated surprisal, entropy, summary statistics (Chapter 6). Distinctive prediction: predictive variables improve held-out prediction beyond preference and acoustic features. Disconfirmed if: they do not, or effects fail to replicate across performances and groups.
M3 — Social coordination. Explanation: participation and interpersonal coordination. Predictors: participation type, coordination indices with surrogate-controlled synchrony metrics (Chapter 11), perceived closeness. Distinctive prediction: social variables improve prediction beyond shared stimulus, individual exposure and measured bodily movement. Disconfirmed if: they add no reproducible value.
M4 — Specified interactions. Explanation: preregistered interactions among acoustic, predictive, social and contextual variables, for example acoustic features × role, surprisal × familiarity, participation × prior legitimacy. Distinctive prediction: the limited, theory-specified interaction set improves held-out prediction beyond the corresponding main-effects model. Constraint: interactions are fixed before confirmatory analysis; no interaction is added after inspecting results.
M5 — Cultural legitimacy and ritual context. Explanation: role, tradition, setting and prior-measured legitimacy. Distinctive prediction: contextual variables improve prediction beyond preference, acoustics, predictive terms and social participation. Constraint: tested against a fair baseline containing essential confounders, not against an artificially weak model.
M6 — Bottom-up entrainment. Explanation: rhythmic stimulation and physiological or neural entrainment. Predictors: entrainment-related measures (for example, coupling between rhythmic stimulus frequency and physiological or movement rhythms, where measurable). Distinctive prediction: entrainment measures predict experiential onset or maintenance beyond preference, context and other measured mechanisms. Caveat: relevance to Gnawa and Issawa settings must be established independently; evidence from shamanic practitioners cannot be presumed to transfer.
5.3 The nested comparison ladder
To address overlap between acoustic and predictive predictors, models are evaluated on a prespecified ladder:
• L0 Baseline: core confounders and individual preference.
• L1 Acoustic: add preregistered musical features.
• L2 Predictive: add participant-validated surprisal and entropy summaries.
• L3 Social: add prespecified participation and coordination variables.
• L4 Contextual: add prior-measured legitimacy and other cultural moderators.
• L5 Mechanism: add the specified M4 interactions and M6 entrainment predictors.
• L6 Dynamic: compare against the state-dependent generative model (Chapter 8).
At each step the report gives incremental held-out predictive gain with its uncertainty, calibration and complexity. The ordering is an analytic convention, not a causal sequence. Because predictor blocks share information, conclusions depend on order; therefore a sensitivity analysis repeats the ladder under alternative prespecified block orders and reports the range of each block's incremental contribution (an order-averaged contribution may be reported as a secondary summary).
5.4 Predictive criteria
• Primary: expected log predictive density on held-out data (performance-level and future-phase partitions, Chapter 12), with uncertainty in model differences.
• Secondary: calibration of predictive intervals; stacking weights; information criteria as supplementary checks only. None of these rescues a badly specified model.
• Practical threshold: a smallest effect of interest in predictive gain (δ_min) is preregistered; improvements below δ_min are not interpreted as evidence for added complexity.
5.5 Decision rules
• If M2's block adds less than δ_min beyond L1, M2 has not shown distinctive value.
• If M4's interactions add less than δ_min beyond the main-effects model, the simpler model is retained.
• If context (L4) yields the largest robust improvement, the framework acknowledges a contextual explanation rather than protecting a musical mechanism.
• If no model reliably outperforms L0, that result is reported as such.
5.6 Limits of interpretation
Observational predictive superiority does not establish causal superiority. Models can discriminate predictive explanations better than they can establish what would happen if a ritual context were experimentally changed.
5.7 Hypothesis registry
• H1 Non-linear surprisal: the outcome varies non-monotonically with participant-validated surprisal (Chapter 7).
• H2 Context dependence: musical features do not predict outcomes equally across settings, populations and experience categories.
• H3 Role moderation: role explains variation beyond acoustics and preference.
• H4 Expectation and familiarity: both influence some outcomes and are distinguished (familiarity is exposure; expectation is a model-based quantity).
• H5 Social participation: collective participation adds prediction beyond shared stimulus, coordinated movement and breathing.
• H6 Partial convergence: selected dimensions (absorption, intensity, connectedness) show partial similarity across traditions, conditional on measurement invariance.
• H7 Dissociation: attentional performance, absorption, emotional intensity, physiological arousal and spiritual interpretation do not covary strongly.
• H8 State dependence: response to matched input depends on preceding latent state (ρ > 0 beyond trends and autocorrelation artefacts).
• H9 Hysteresis: entry and exit conditions differ (θ↑ > θ↓; Chapter 8).
• H10 Entrainment: entrainment measures predict entry beyond M1–M5 predictors.
Chapter 6. Operationalizing Predictive Processing
6.1 Why operationalization is necessary
Predictive processing is too flexible unless the quantities are specified before outcomes are observed. This chapter fixes three predictors, the sequence-model requirement, a mandatory validation stage and falsification criteria.
6.2 Surprisal and entropy
For musical event x_t:
S_t = − log P(x_t | x_<t, M_i)
H_t = − Σ_x P(x | x_<t, M_i) · log P(x | x_<t, M_i)
where x_<t is the preceding musical context and M_i is a sequence model intended to approximate participant i's learned expectations. Surprisal measures the unexpectedness of the observed event; entropy measures uncertainty over possible next events before observation. They are distinct and neither automatically measures what a listener consciously expects.
6.3 Rhythmic complexity
A third, separate predictor: a preregistered rhythmic-complexity index derived from onset timing, inter-onset intervals or deviation from an identified pulse or pattern (candidate indices include syncopation measures, inter-onset-interval entropy and pattern-recurrence measures). Surprisal and rhythmic complexity are not merged into an improvised "complexity score".
6.4 Enculturated sequence models
The sequence model must not assume that a Western-trained corpus approximates a Gnawa or Issawa listener's expectations. Requirements:
• Representation. Highly improvised or polyrhythmic performances may require audio-derived events or multiple concurrent rhythmic streams (for example guembri pattern, percussion layers, vocal line) rather than a single symbolic transcription. Representation choices are made with performers and ethnomusicological advisers and fixed before outcome analysis.
• Model family. Variable-order Markov or multiple-viewpoint models of the kind used in music-cognition research (for example IDyOM-style approaches [V]) or modern sequence models trained on tradition-specific corpora. The family is chosen in advance.
• Training corpus. Tradition-specific, with provenance documented and community permissions for recordings respected (Chapter 14). If no adequate corpus can be assembled ethically, the confirmatory test of M2 is deferred.
• Participant adaptation. M_i is parameterized as a mixture of a tradition-level model and a generic-music model with participant-specific weight w_i estimated from expectation tasks: P_i(x) = w_i · P_tradition(x) + (1 − w_i) · P_generic(x). Role and familiarity are expected to shift w_i.
6.5 Mandatory validation stage
Before confirmatory testing of M2, the sequence model must pass validation using participant-appropriate tasks:
• expectation or continuation judgments;
• tapping or prediction tasks where culturally and musically appropriate;
• familiarity assessments and interviews on expected developments;
• evaluation on held-out sequences or excerpts.
Tasks must avoid imposing unfamiliar notation or laboratory assumptions. Acceptance criteria (for example a minimum correspondence between model-predicted and participant-expected continuations, and better held-out likelihood than a null baseline) are preregistered. If a defensible model cannot be established, M2's confirmatory test is deferred; generic audio complexity may still be studied but is not described as a validated measure of listener expectation.
6.6 Summary variables
The summary S_ip relevant to an outcome is fixed in advance from a small set (for example window mean, window maximum, proportion of events above a preregistered threshold, variability). Choosing the summary after seeing outcomes is prohibited.
6.7 Predictions specific to entry and maintenance
If perceptual decoupling during trance is real in these settings [H], stimulus-linked surprisal should predict entry into higher absorption better than it predicts maintenance, since maintained states may become less dependent on conscious processing of the stimulus. This is a testable possibility, not an established property of Gnawa or Issawa ritual.
6.8 Falsification of M2
M2 is weakened if its predictors fail to improve held-out prediction beyond preference and acoustic features, if expected associations fail to replicate across performances or groups, or if effects disappear under reasonable sensitivity analyses. A failed operationalization is distinguished from a failed mechanism: a poor sequence model does not refute predictive processing generally, but it prevents the study from claiming to have tested the participant's predictions.
Chapter 7. The Inverted-U Hypothesis
7.1 Statement (H1)
Over a lower range of participant-validated surprisal the target outcome may increase, and over a higher range decrease, with the turning point estimated only within the supported range. Rhythmic complexity is a separate secondary predictor. The inverted-U is a candidate model to be compared against linear and no-effect alternatives, not an assumed law.
7.2 Candidate model
Y_ip = β₀ + β₁ S_ip + β₂ S_ip² + γᵀ Z_ip + u_p + v_i + ε_ip
where Y_ip is the outcome for participant i in performance p, S_ip the preregistered surprisal summary, Z_ip specified moderators and confounders, u_p and v_i performance- and participant-level effects, and ε_ip residual variation. For bounded or ordinal outcomes an appropriate link (for example ordinal logistic) replaces the identity link.
7.3 Why a quadratic term is insufficient
A significant quadratic coefficient can arise from a monotonic but curved relationship or from a skewed distribution; it does not by itself demonstrate a turning point within the data. Confirmatory inference therefore uses a preregistered formal U-shape procedure, such as the two-lines approach (Simonsohn, 2018 [V]) or a formal slope test, with a Bayesian alternative (for example a flexible smooth with the posterior probability of a sign change inside the supported range).
7.4 Conditions for support
Evidence for an inverted U requires:
1. a positive slope in the prespecified lower region;
2. a negative slope in the prespecified upper region;
3. a turning point inside a sufficiently observed range;
4. enough observations on both sides of the turning point to distinguish the pattern from noise.
If the range is inadequate or either slope is unsupported, the conclusion is inconclusive, not evidence of an inverted U.
7.5 Individual differences
The turning point may differ between participants and roles. A hierarchical extension allows participant- and role-specific turning points (random quadratic coefficients), with the preregistered primary test at the level of the population mean and a secondary report of heterogeneity.
7.6 Comparison with alternatives
The inverted-U model is compared with a linear model and a null model by held-out prediction as well as by the formal U-test. A better in-sample fit is not accepted as evidence.
7.7 Relation to dynamics
The static inverted-U relates the outcome to input; the dynamic model (Chapter 8) allows the optimal input range to depend on current state. If the dynamic model is supported, the static inverted-U is expected to appear attenuated in pooled data because it averages over states. This is a further testable relationship between H1 and H8.
Chapter 8. The Dynamic Generative Model
8.1 Rationale
Static regression models relate an outcome to current inputs. If experience is a state that carries over from moment to moment, the same input may have different effects depending on where the participant already is. Only a model with an explicit state can represent this. The dynamic model is therefore the dissertation's principal theoretical device, and its value is judged by whether it predicts held-out temporal data better than static alternatives.
8.2 Initial model: one latent absorption dimension
The first model uses a single latent absorption dimension rather than forcing observations into four discrete states. Let a_it be participant i's latent absorption at phase or time index t; m_it musical predictors (including S_it, H_it and rhythmic complexity); r_i participant characteristics and role; and c_ip contextual variables for performance p.
State equation:
a_it = α_i + ρ · a_i,t−1 + βᵀ m_it + δᵀ c_ip + ηᵀ (r_i ⊗ m_it) + ε_it
where ρ is state persistence, ⊗ denotes preregistered interactions, α_i is a participant intercept and ε_it is unexplained state variation.
Measurement equation:
y_itk = ν_k + λ_k · a_it + e_itk
where y_itk is indicator k, ν_k its intercept, λ_k its loading and e_itk measurement error. The likelihood and link depend on whether an indicator is continuous, ordinal, binary or physiological (for example an ordinal logistic link for brief self-report items; a Gaussian or heavier-tailed likelihood for physiological summaries).
These equations are a proposed starting point. They do not establish that absorption is one-dimensional or that the parameters are identifiable from the intended data.
8.3 State dependence and hysteresis
State dependence (H8). ρ > 0 after controlling for time trends, fatigue, ritual phase and measurement drift. Equivalent observable form: for time points with closely matched musical input m, outcomes differ systematically with the preceding state.
Hysteresis (H9). A regime extension makes hysteresis precise. Let r_it ∈ {L, H} denote a low or high absorption regime, with switching rule:
r_it switches L → H when a_it exceeds an entry threshold θ↑
r_it switches H → L when a_it falls below an exit threshold θ↓
The system exhibits hysteresis if and only if θ↑ > θ↓; the hysteresis width is Δθ = θ↑ − θ↓. In words, the conditions required to enter the high state are more demanding than those required to remain in it. Observable implications: (i) the distribution of musical and bodily conditions at entry differs from that at exit; (ii) for identical input, the high-state response depends on r_i,t−1; (iii) the entry and exit paths are asymmetric.
Hysteresis is a stronger claim than state dependence. If Δθ is not distinguishable from zero, the model reduces to a simple autoregressive state model and H9 is not supported.
8.4 Distinctive predictions relative to static models
1. Lagged-state effect (ρ > 0) survives controls that static models with trend terms cannot absorb.
2. Matched-input contrasts: pairs of time points with similar m but different preceding state differ in outcome in the predicted direction.
3. Entry and exit conditions differ (Δθ > 0).
4. Held-out forward prediction (leave-future-out) improves relative to L0–L5.
5. Pooled static inverted-U effects are attenuated relative to state-conditional effects (Chapter 7.7).
8.5 Entry, maintenance and exit sub-models
In discrete time, entry, maintenance and exit can be modelled as hazards:
h_entry(t) = P(r_t = H | r_t−1 = L, x_t) = logistic(φ_0 + φᵀ x_t)
h_exit(t)  = P(r_t = L | r_t−1 = H, x_t) = logistic(ψ_0 + ψᵀ x_t)
where x_t contains musical, predictive, bodily, social and contextual predictors. The entrainment account (M6) may predict that entry hazards depend on rhythmic stimulation more strongly than exit or maintenance hazards. If perceptual decoupling occurs, maintenance may depend less on stimulus-linked surprisal. These differences between φ and ψ are directly testable and constitute H10 and the entry/maintenance prediction of Chapter 6.7.
8.6 Role-dependent priors
Role, familiarity and tradition enter as hierarchical priors, for example α_i ~ Normal(α_role(i), σ²_α) and role-specific loadings on musical predictors. Role-dependent expectation enters via the participant-specific weight w_i of the sequence model (Chapter 6.4).
8.7 Ritual phase structure
Ritual performances (for example all-night ceremonies) have phase structure that is itself a driver of state and of music. Phase is represented as a covariate defined ethnographically, or as a partially observed latent phase variable (for example a hidden Markov layer anchored to ethnographic phase annotations). Phase definitions and validation rules come from Phase 2 and the pilot, not from the model fit.
8.8 Identifiability and simulation
Identifiability is not assumed. Standard results for linear-Gaussian dynamic factor models suggest that a single latent factor with at least three indicators of non-zero loading per time point, a scale constraint (for example fixing one loading) and sufficient time points per participant yields identified parameters up to sign and scale [V]. These results do not automatically extend to the ordinal-link, regime-switching or hazard extensions; for these, identifiability is evaluated by simulation (Appendix E):
• parameter recovery of ρ, β, λ_k, θ↑, θ↓ and hazard coefficients from simulated data with realistic numbers of participants, performances, time points, missingness and measurement noise;
• weak-identification diagnostics (posterior contraction, prior sensitivity);
• the ability to distinguish state dependence from simple time trends and autocorrelated noise;
• the minimum number of transitions of each type needed to estimate Δθ.
8.9 Failure and simplification rules
If the dynamic model does not improve held-out forward prediction, or its parameters cannot be recovered in simulation, it is simplified (for example to the autoregressive model without regimes, or to a static model) rather than defended by adding latent variables. A multi-dimensional extension (mixed-membership profiles over T1–T4) is considered only if the one-dimensional model passes these tests and ethnography indicates that dimensional structure is necessary.
Part III — The Research Programme
Chapter 9. Two-Tier Design
9.1 Ethnographic discovery (Phase 2)
The first empirical phase is qualitative and descriptive. Methods: participant observation, interviews with performers, practitioners, initiates and attendees, musical analysis with community advisers, and historical research. Deliverables:
• a glossary of local terms for devotion, emotional release, trance and possession, and how participants distinguish them;
• a map of ritual phases, roles and repertoires, with local rules for participation;
• an account of what the music is intended to accomplish in each setting and which structures participants find meaningful;
• a list of which experiences can be studied ethically and validly outside ritual settings, and which cannot;
• a feasibility matrix specifying, for each planned measure, whether the community and participants find it acceptable;
• a positionality statement (insider, outsider, participant, observer) and its implications.
Sacred performances are not experimental resources available for unrestricted manipulation (Chapter 14).
9.2 Tier A — laboratory study of T1 and T2
Purpose. Investigate devotional and musical absorption under controlled, ethically appropriate conditions.
Conditions (included only when they preserve the construct and do not misrepresent a sacred practice):
1. solitary listening to a familiar recording;
2. solitary listening to a matched non-ritual musical control;
3. collective listening without active participation;
4. collective participation, where ethically and practically appropriate;
5. selected manipulations of musical predictability or structure, constructed with performers' agreement.
Design features.
• Within-person design with counterbalancing (for example Latin-square ordering) and washout; carry-over and order effects modelled.
• Stimulus sampling: excerpts are treated as random effects so that inference generalizes beyond the specific excerpts.
• Manipulation checks: verify that manipulated surprisal or complexity differs as intended according to the validated sequence model and that participants perceive it.
• Demand characteristics: neutral instructions, concealed hypotheses where ethical, expectation measured and modelled.
• Outcome timing: brief post-condition reports and physiological measurements; continuous self-report during intense listening is avoided when it would disrupt absorption.
Measures. Attention, affect, absorption, preference, familiarity, expectation, movement, breathing and physiological arousal.
Inferential scope. Manipulated musical features may support causal claims about measured outcomes under tested laboratory conditions, subject to valid manipulation, measurement and assignment. Laboratory findings are not treated as demonstrations of ritual trance or possession.
9.3 Tier B — naturalistic field study of T3 and T4
Purpose. Document naturally occurring ritual experiences with community consultation and safeguards appropriate to each setting.
Design features.
• Sampling of performances stratified by tradition, setting, ritual type and community, with enough independent performances to support the planned hierarchy (Chapter 12).
• Participants are recruited without pressure; roles are recorded; no one is randomized into possession or pressed to enter altered states; sacred rituals are never disrupted to create experimental conditions.
• Recording: ritual phase, role, participation, repertoire familiarity, environmental conditions, locally recognized experience categories, and relevant physiological exposures where measurement is acceptable. Possible instruments include unobtrusive heart-rate and respiration sensors, accelerometers and audio, subject to the feasibility matrix.
• Previous-night sleep and fatigue captured by short diary or interview.
• Experience measures obtained at phase boundaries or after the ritual in participant-defined terms, avoiding interruption.
• Natural experiments (for example a change of repertoire, venue or lead performer) are used only when they arise without researcher interference and when an identification strategy is defensible.
Inferential scope. Contextual associations, temporal analyses, within-person comparisons and carefully bounded predictive evaluation. Causal claims require an additional defensible identification strategy and cannot be inferred from observational correlations alone.
9.4 Linking the tiers
The tiers inform a common theoretical framework without treating their measurements or inferential strengths as interchangeable. A triangulation matrix records, for each hypothesis, which tier tests it, what it can license, and what remains untested. The laboratory cannot inherit the field study's cultural meaning; the field study cannot inherit the laboratory's causal interpretation. Pooling data across tiers in a single inferential model is prohibited unless an explicit tier-level hierarchy and a measurement-equivalence argument are specified in advance.
9.5 Selection and generalization
People who respond strongly to spiritual music are more likely to participate in rituals, and ritual specialists are not representative of attendees. Selection is addressed by recording role and history, by modelling role as a stratifying variable, and by restricting claims to the sampled communities and roles.
Chapter 10. Measurement Development and Comparability
10.1 Emic-to-etic process
1. Ethnographic discovery: document locally meaningful distinctions in participants' own terms.
2. Item development: write candidate items from participant language and relevant existing measures, without presupposing identical categories across traditions.
3. Cognitive interviewing: test whether participants interpret items as intended and whether items are culturally appropriate.
4. Pilot validation: examine reliability, response distributions, missingness, test–retest behaviour where appropriate, and convergence with related measures.
5. Invariance testing: assess whether the measurement structure is sufficiently comparable across traditions before comparing latent means or effect sizes.
10.2 Language and adaptation
Instruments are developed in the languages of participation (for example Moroccan Arabic and Amazigh languages as locally relevant, and Standard Arabic for liturgical vocabulary), using translation, independent back-translation and cognitive interviewing. Sacred terms are not translated away; where participants' words have no equivalent, the original term is retained and glossed.
10.3 Candidate existing instruments
Existing self-report instruments for trait absorption, affect, awe, flow, perceived social closeness and musical reward are candidates for adaptation (for example the Tellegen Absorption Scale family [V adaptation status]). Adaptation requires the steps above; imported scales are not assumed valid.
10.4 Indicators for the latent absorption model
The dynamic model requires at least three indicators per time point (Chapter 8.8). Candidate indicators: brief phase-level self-report items; physiological arousal summaries; behavioural indices (for example movement variability) and, in Tier A, brief attention probes. Indicator choice is justified by ethnographic acceptability and by the invariance and reliability evidence from the pilot.
10.5 Invariance
For ordinal items, assessment includes configural invariance (same structure), loading (metric) invariance and threshold (scalar) invariance across tradition and, where relevant, role. If invariance fails:
• use partial-invariance models where a defensible subset of items is invariant;
• use tradition-specific measures with explicitly limited comparisons;
• or report that the cross-tradition comparison is not identified by available measurements.
Approximate-invariance methods (for example alignment-type approaches [V]) may be used as sensitivity analyses.
10.6 Response biases
Retrospective reports are affected by memory, expectation, social desirability and difficulty verbalizing complex states. Mitigations: short intervals between experience and report, neutral interviewers where possible, parallel physiological and behavioural indicators, and modelling of expectation as a predictor. Self-report and physiology are never treated as interchangeable (H7).
10.7 Treatment of T1–T4
T1–T4 remain provisional analytic labels. A common questionnaire does not make the categories universal. Cross-cultural convergence (H6) is a hypothesis tested subject to invariance, not a premise.
Chapter 11. Synchrony and Embodied Confounds
11.1 The problem
Apparent physiological synchrony can arise from a shared stimulus, synchronized movement, common breathing or common environmental conditions. It is not sufficient evidence of interpersonal coupling, emotional unity or spiritual connection.
11.2 Synchrony metrics and surrogates
Candidate metrics include windowed cross-correlation, wavelet coherence and cross-recurrence measures. For each, the protocol requires:
• Shuffled-pair surrogates: the observed synchrony of real pairs is compared with synchrony of pairs drawn across performances or non-interacting individuals.
• Temporal misalignment surrogates: signals are shifted or phase-randomized to preserve spectral properties while destroying alignment.
• Sensitivity analyses across metrics, window lengths and detrending choices.
11.3 Separating common drivers from residual coordination
Where feasible the analysis separates synchrony attributable to common external input (stimulus timing, shared movement, shared breathing) from residual coordination, for example by partialling common drivers from the signals before computing coupling. Even residual synchrony does not automatically establish social bonding, a neural mechanism or spiritual connection. Subjective social closeness is measured separately and never inferred from synchrony.
11.4 Embodied and environmental variables
When ethically and practically appropriate the field protocol records:
• sleep duration and quality, fatigue and time of day;
• breathing rate, sustained vocalization, movement and posture;
• heat, ventilation and relevant airborne exposures (for example incense);
• fasting, food and fluid intake;
• ritual duration, preceding activity and phase;
• other physiological or environmental factors justified by the question; medications or other exposures only when justified, consented to and necessary.
11.5 Assigning causal roles
Each variable is assigned a role before analysis: confounder, mediator, consequence of participation, or integral part of the ritual. Assignments come from the DAG (Chapter 4.3) and ethnography.
Example. Breathing may be affected by participation and may in turn affect absorption. Estimating the total effect of participation requires not adjusting for breathing; estimating an association independent of respiratory change adjusts for it. Adjusting would remove part of the pathway under investigation if the practice itself changes breathing. The analysis therefore states the estimand explicitly and reports separate total-effect and conditional analyses only where their assumptions are defensible.
11.6 Rival explanations
Music is never inferred to have caused an altered experience merely because the experience coincided with music. Fatigue, exertion, fasting, expectation, heat and ritual meaning are serious rival explanations; they are included as modelled variables, not footnotes.
11.7 Duty of care
Some ritual conditions (heat, exertion, sustained vocalization, fasting) carry physical risks. Researchers do not encourage participants to push beyond comfort, monitor wellbeing within the limits set by the community, and have a protocol for responding to distress, consistent with the community's own care practices.
Chapter 12. Statistical Analysis Plan
12.1 Unit and hierarchy
The primary unit is the participant–episode (or phase), as fixed by the measurement protocol. Episodes are nested in participants, performances, groups and communities, with levels included only where the sampling design supports them. Not every timestamped observation counts as an independent replication; independent replication is counted at the level of performances and communities.
12.2 Hierarchical model skeleton
Bayesian multilevel models are the preferred starting point because they represent partial pooling and uncertainty across clustered data:
y_ipgc ~ p(y | θ_ipgc)
θ_ipgc = f(x_ipgc, z_ipgc) + v_i + u_p + w_g + s_c
v_i ~ Normal(0, σ²_participant),  u_p ~ Normal(0, σ²_performance), ...
Additional random slopes are included only where the design supports them. Priors are weakly informative, justified by prior predictive checks, and subjected to prior-sensitivity analysis. When the number of communities is small, no model can manufacture cross-community generalizability: community is then treated as a stratum, variance between communities is not estimated as if well identified, and this limitation is reported explicitly.
12.3 Outcome-specific likelihoods
Ordinal items use ordinal-logistic links; binary entry/exit events use logistic or hazard likelihoods; physiological summaries use Gaussian or heavier-tailed likelihoods after preregistered preprocessing; counts use appropriate count models.
12.4 Validation
• Leave-one-performance-out prediction to new performances, where its assumptions are defensible (performances are exchangeable within stratum).
• Leave-future-out (rolling-origin) validation within performances, evaluating whether models predict later phases using only earlier information. This respects the sequential and non-exchangeable structure of ritual phases.
• Community-level holdouts only if enough communities are available.
• Random splits of adjacent time points are avoided wherever temporal dependence could leak information.
• Performance-level and future-phase validation answer different questions and are reported separately.
• Fast approximate leave-one-out methods (for example PSIS-LOO [V]) are not used where dependence invalidates their assumptions; exact refits are used instead.
12.5 Comparison metrics
Primary: expected log predictive density differences with uncertainty. Secondary: calibration of predictive intervals, stacking weights, information criteria as supplementary checks. The practical threshold δ_min is preregistered. An improvement smaller than δ_min, or whose interval comfortably includes zero, is not interpreted as support for additional complexity.
12.6 Simulation-based design analysis
Sample size is justified by simulation, not by conventional participant-count rules. Simulations (Appendix E) specify plausible effect sizes, variance components, missingness, numbers of participants, performances, groups and communities, and evaluate:
1. parameter recovery and identifiability;
2. interval coverage and uncertainty calibration;
3. model discrimination (the probability that the preregistered decision rule selects the true generating model);
4. sensitivity to smaller effects, stronger clustering and fewer independent performances;
5. the ability to distinguish state dependence from time trends and autocorrelation.
The design is chosen according to the scientific decision it must support.
12.7 Preregistration and multiplicity
Before confirmatory analysis the following are frozen: outcomes, estimands, predictor sets and summary variables, sequence-model validation criteria, ladder order and alternative orders, validation splits, δ_min, decision rules and the limited set of confirmatory hypotheses (Appendix B). Exploratory analyses are labelled and independently validated before being treated as mechanisms. Multiplicity is controlled by restricting confirmatory hypotheses, by hierarchical shrinkage, and by reporting all preregistered comparisons.
12.8 Missing data are informative
In ritual settings, missing reports can be informative about state: a participant deep in trance may not respond, or may not be asked. Missingness is therefore modelled (for example with selection or pattern-mixture sensitivity analyses) rather than assumed ignorable, and reported with its likely direction of bias.
12.9 Robustness and reporting
Sensitivity analyses vary sequence-model family, summary variable, window length, synchrony metric and priors. Analysis code, simulations and preregistration are archived; reporting follows preregistered structure with deviations documented. Community restrictions on raw data (Chapter 14) are respected; synthetic or aggregated data and code remain open.
Part IV — Evaluation and Outlook
Chapter 13. Falsification, Threats to Validity and Limitations
13.1 Revision rules
The framework is revised or narrowed if:
• acoustic features and preference explain outcomes without reliable incremental contribution from predictive terms;
• social variables add no reproducible predictive value beyond shared stimulus and measured bodily activity;
• cultural context does not improve held-out prediction under a sufficiently informative design;
• the entrainment model fails to predict entry or maintenance beyond simpler alternatives;
• the dynamic model fails parameter-recovery checks or does not predict future phases better than static models;
• proposed cross-tradition comparisons fail invariance checks;
• apparent inverted-U relationships lack the preregistered directional evidence;
• effect estimates remain too uncertain to distinguish competing explanations.
13.2 Interpretation of null and mixed results
Failure to reject a null model does not establish a rival model. Inadequate measurement, insufficient independent performances and limited community coverage may leave a result unresolved; this category is preregistered with explicit criteria (for example, a decision interval that includes both zero and δ_min). Null findings do not show that spiritual music lacks personal, religious, cultural or social significance, and failure to find a universal physiological signature does not refute the existence of spiritual experience; the theory does not require one.
13.3 Threats to validity
• Internal: unmeasured fatigue and ritual-phase effects mimicking state dependence; demand characteristics; carry-over in Tier A; reverse causation in legitimacy ratings (mitigated by prior measurement).
• Construct: non-comparable meanings across traditions; imperfect sequence models of expectation; self-report limits; categories imposed by researchers.
• Statistical conclusion: few independent performances; weak identification of latent dynamics; order dependence of the ladder; informative missingness; forking paths (mitigated by preregistration).
• External: self-selected participants; small number of communities; restriction to Moroccan traditions; transfer from non-Moroccan evidence.
13.4 Limitations
• This is a conceptual and design contribution; no empirical findings are claimed.
• Several cited sources are conference abstracts, talks or secondary summaries and are flagged [V]; none is used as stand-alone support.
• Access to ritual settings depends on community decisions; some experiences may remain unrecorded, and a scientifically useful study may have to leave them so.
• The framework could be undermined by the possibility that essential determinants of these experiences are contextual and not capturable by the proposed variables.
• AI collaboration. This dissertation was developed with AI collaborative assistance. The human author remains responsible for verifying all citations and claims; AI-assisted text is not a substitute for primary-source verification.
Chapter 14. Ethics, Positionality and Data Governance
14.1 Consent in layers
Research in sacred settings requires community consultation and consent appropriate to the setting, as well as individual informed consent, with the right to withdraw and the right not to participate in any measurement procedure. Consent is ongoing and revisited at each ritual, particularly where altered agency may occur.
14.2 Dignity and safety
No measurement is treated as more important than the safety, dignity or autonomy of participants. Recording of possession-related or trance-related experiences requires explicit safeguards and may be declined by communities or individuals. People who attend rituals seeking healing are not subjects to be exploited; recruitment avoids pressure and respects local understandings of care.
14.3 Positionality
Researchers document their role (insider, outsider, participant, observer), how it shapes access, interpretation and behaviour, and how disagreement between researchers' framing and participants' framing is handled. Participant interpretations are documented as meaningful in themselves and are neither endorsed as scientific explanations nor dismissed as error.
14.4 Data sovereignty and open knowledge
The project is committed to open access to its code, analysis plans, simulations and syntheses. This commitment can conflict with community restrictions on recordings. The resolution is tiered: methods, code, preregistrations, synthetic data and aggregated results are open; raw audio, video and physiological recordings are held and shared only under terms decided with communities. Established data-governance frameworks for communities (for example the CARE principles for Indigenous data governance, applied by analogy where appropriate [V applicability]) inform the agreements. Communities determine access, retention and secondary use.
14.5 Benefit, return and representation
Results are returned to participating communities in accessible language and in their languages. Commercial exploitation of sacred performances (a concern raised in ethnographic work on the global marketplace for Gnawa music) is avoided, and benefit-sharing is discussed in advance. Reporting avoids exoticization and avoids reducing practices to mechanisms; Levels 1 and 2 are presented together.
14.6 Review
The programme requires approval by the relevant research ethics body and, where available, community-level review, before any human-subjects data collection.
Chapter 15. Research Roadmap
Each phase ends with a decision gate; later phases are not started if the gate is failed.
• Phase 1 — Literature and construct audit. Verify primary sources (Appendix F), grade evidence, define target constructs, search for Issawa-specific work systematically. Gate: verified evidence table; revised hypotheses.
• Phase 2 — Ethnographic discovery. Local terms, roles, ritual phases, ethical constraints, feasibility matrix. Gate: community agreements; typology confirmed or revised.
• Phase 3 — Measurement and sequence-model validation. Emic-derived items; cognitive interviewing; expectation tasks; sequence-model acceptance. Gate: validated instruments or deferral of M2.
• Phase 4 — Generative-model simulation. Identifiability, recovery, discrimination, sample design. Gate: design that can support the preregistered decisions, or simplification of the model.
• Phase 5 — Pilot studies. Laboratory pilot for T1/T2; field feasibility for naturally occurring T3/T4. Gate: feasibility, acceptability and measurement quality.
• Phase 6 — Preregistered confirmatory research. Freeze outcomes, estimands, comparisons, validation splits and decision rules before analysis. Gate: completed analysis per the plan.
• Phase 7 — Replication and comparison. Extend to further performances and traditions only where measurement comparability and access justify it.
Principal risks. Community access denied or limited (mitigation: Tier A and archival analyses, ethnography-first); sequence model cannot be validated (mitigation: defer M2, study generic complexity separately); too few independent performances (mitigation: simplify hypotheses, report limits); latent model not identified (mitigation: simplify); measurement non-invariance (mitigation: tradition-specific measures).
Chapter 16. Contribution and Discussion
16.1 What the dissertation contributes
The dissertation does not claim a universal explanation of spirituality, nor that different religions express one metaphysical truth, nor that predictive processing explains trance, nor merely that context matters. Those claims are either metaphysical, already widely held, or both. Its contribution is a framework in which acoustic, predictive, social, cultural and entrainment explanations are compared under shared definitions and out-of-sample tests; in which temporal dependence and possible hysteresis are modelled explicitly; and in which the limits of laboratory, field and cross-cultural inference are stated in advance.
16.2 What would count as success
Success includes learning that simpler models suffice, that context dominates, that different traditions rely on different mechanisms, or that some apparently universal patterns are culturally specific. A result in which no model beats the baseline is a legitimate result.
16.3 Mechanism and meaning
The framework preserves a distinction between measurable processes, lived meaning and unresolved metaphysical questions. It neither reduces spirituality to mechanism nor takes the intensity of experience as proof of any theological claim. This neutrality is a methodological constraint, not a denial of the significance participants find in their practice.
16.4 Future directions
Multi-dimensional latent profiles over T1–T4; acoustic properties of ritual sites as an additional context variable (a related line of research exists in the neurobiology-of-altered-states literature [E4][V]); extension to other Moroccan and non-Moroccan traditions subject to invariance; integration of ethnographic narrative with quantitative models.



